# Build output
bin/
# `go build ./cmd/thn` drops a bare `thn` in the cwd. The ignores below only
# covered the explicitly-named release artefacts, so a 20 MB binary sat
# untracked at the repo root waiting for a `git add -A`. Match any build
# output of the gateway binary, whatever it is named or which OS it is for.
/thn
/thn.exe
thn-linux-amd64
thn-darwin-arm64

# Go workspace / vendored deps
vendor/

# Local state, never commit a real gateway's database or config
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# Editor noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp

# Next.js console (ui/). The Go module ignores it: no .go files in that tree.
# node_modules and .next must never be committed - both are large and the lockfile
# already pins the exact dependency set.
ui/node_modules/
ui/.next/
ui/out/
ui/*.tsbuildinfo

# NOTE on ui/package-lock.json
#
# Do not run `npm install` on the gateway box to "fix" dependencies. `npm ci`
# installs exactly what the lockfile pins and never rewrites it.
#
# `npm install` rewrites this file, and two npm versions disagree about its
# contents: the lockfile carries `libc` fields for Linux-only optional
# dependencies that npm 10.9 writes and npm 10.8 does not. A developer on
# 10.9 and the box on 10.8 will each strip the other's fields, which leaves
# each with a dirty lockfile and makes the next `git pull` refuse to merge.
# Regenerate it deliberately, on one machine, and commit the result.
